Matches of an expression-statement pattern such as `foo();` that occur inside
a larger statement via implicit deep expression matching now report the range
of the matched subexpression instead of the range of the whole statement.
For such findings: match ranges and fingerprints shift, `as` metavariable
bindings and `fix:` rewrites apply to the subexpression instead of the whole
statement, range-based conditions in composite rules (for example
`pattern-inside`) see the narrowed range, and a statement that contains
several matching subexpressions now yields one finding per subexpression
instead of a single whole-statement finding.
